   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to allowing persons\napplying for or receiving public assistance to be interviewed by phone",
    "basePrintNo": "S3223",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3223-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 28,
       "fullName": "Liz Krueger",
       "imgName": "401_liz_krueger.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 401,
       "sessionMemberId": 1820,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "KRUEGER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §134-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3223\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to allowing persons\napplying for or receiving public assistance to be interviewed by phone\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would provide local social services districts with the flexi-\nbility to interview applicants for public assistance via telephone.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 3 of section 143-a of the social services\nlaw, as amended by chapter 219 of the laws of 1973, to provide local\nsocial services districts with the option to conduct required interviews\nvia telephone at a time and in a manner provided through regulations of\nthe department.\n\nSection 2 sets forth an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York State Social Services law requires local social services\ndistricts to interview public assistance applicants as part of the\nreview and certification process.\n\nUnder a waiver from USDA, the NYS Office of Temporary and Disability\nAssistance (OTDA) has authorized local social services districts to\nconduct SNAP eligibility and recertification interviews via telephone\nsince 2011. In 2015, the waiver was extended to allow the New York City\nDepartment of Social Services (DSS) to conduct unscheduled or \"on-de-\nmand\" interviews via telephone. By September 2019, nearly 90% of all\nSNAP applications were submitted and processed remotely. Since the onset\nof COVID-19 in early 2020, 99% of all SNAP applications have submitted\nonline and the corresponding interviews were conducted telephonically.\n\nWith regard to cash assistance applications, OTDA has historically\nrequired local districts to conduct in-person interviews, though has\ngranted temporary waivers allowing districts to conduct telephone inter-\nviews thus physically distancing applicants, clients and staff to\nprevent the spread of COVID-19. The waiver of in-person interview\nrequirements for cash assistance applicants coincided with NYC DSS\nreceiving double the amount of applications for assistance. This has\nallowed NYC DSS to process 85% of all applications online and conduct\ntelephone interviews of some 166,000 applicants. Thus, the new surge of\napplications for cash assistance were handled in the same dignified\nmanner as applicants for SNAP, a federally-regulated program.\n\nThe surge in applications for SNAP, cash assistance and other public\nassistance programs due to COVID-19 is not specific to New York City;\nnor is the need for districts to have flexibility to accommodate the\nneeds of applicants in a safe, efficient manner whether they live in a\ndensely populated metropolis or in a remote area far from the nearest\nlocal social services office.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is new legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
